BODY = """
<h3>Proving that the amyloid is AL</h3>
<p>For years, a monoclonal protein in a patient with systemic amyloid was taken as proof of AL type. Genetic screening of 350 patients diagnosed with AL amyloidosis without a family history found an amyloidogenic mutation in 9.7%, most often in fibrinogen A α-chain or transthyretin, and a quarter of those patients also had a low-grade monoclonal gammopathy [[Hereditary amyloidosis misdiagnosed as AL (Lachmann)]]. The overlap is larger in older patients: a monoclonal gammopathy was found in 39% of patients with wild-type ATTR and 49% with ATTR V122I [[MGUS in ATTR amyloidosis (Phull)]]. Clinical features help but do not type the amyloid; AL, hereditary ATTR and wild-type ATTR behave as different cardiac diseases [[AL vs ATTR cardiac profiles (Rapezzi)]].</p>
<p>The deposit itself has to be typed. Abdominal fat aspiration is the simplest tissue test, with a sensitivity of 80% on routine reading and 93% with careful reading of three smears [[Abdominal fat aspiration (van Gameren)]]. Immunohistochemistry read by an expert pathologist typed most cases [[Immunohistochemistry typing (Schönland)]], but laser microdissection with mass spectrometry, introduced in 2009 with 98% accuracy in a cardiac validation set [[Mass spectrometry typing (Vrana)]], is now the reference method. In 16,175 specimens typed by mass spectrometry, AL accounted for 59.0% and ATTR for 28.4%, followed by ALECT2, AA and heavy chain amyloid, with an organ-by-type map that sets the pretest probability of AL for each biopsy site [[Mass spectrometry typing, 16,175 samples (Dasari)]]. In the kidney, about one amyloid in seven was not immunoglobulin-derived, and immunofluorescence missed 7.3% of light chain cases [[Renal amyloidosis typing, 474 cases (Said)]].</p>
<p>The clone is found with blood and urine tests, and no single test is enough. Immunofixation was more sensitive than the free light chain ratio (96% against 76%), and serum and urine immunofixation together with the free light chain ratio identified the amyloidogenic light chain in every patient [[Free light chains with immunofixation (Palladini)]].</p>

<h3>Cardiac imaging and the boundary with ATTR</h3>
<p>Cardiac MRI showed the typical global subendocardial late enhancement of cardiac amyloid [[Cardiac MRI in cardiac amyloidosis (Maceira)]], but it does not separate AL from ATTR. Bone scintigraphy does so only in part. Myocardial uptake was more than 99% sensitive for cardiac ATTR, and grade 2 or 3 uptake with no monoclonal protein in serum or urine was 100% specific, allowing ATTR to be diagnosed without a biopsy; the false positives came almost entirely from cardiac AL [[Nonbiopsy diagnosis of cardiac ATTR (Gillmore)]]. Half of patients with cardiac AL took up the tracer, usually at low grade [[DPD scintigraphy in AL and ATTR (Hutt)]]. The European cardiology algorithm therefore starts with scintigraphy and the monoclonal protein tests together, and requires tissue typing whenever a monoclonal protein is found [[ESC position statement on cardiac amyloidosis (Garcia-Pavia)]]. For hematologists, this means that the patient with a monoclonal gammopathy and a positive scan needs a biopsy, often endomyocardial, before any treatment.</p>

<h3>Staging: the heart decides</h3>
<p>The Mayo 2004 system used NT-proBNP and troponin to define three stages, with median survival of 26.4, 10.5 and 3.5 months [[Mayo 2004 cardiac staging (Dispenzieri)]]. Stage III proved too broad: among 346 patients in stage III, NT-proBNP above 8500 ng/L and systolic pressure below 100 mm Hg identified a group with a median survival of 3 months, and 42% of all stage III patients died before their first response assessment [[European stage IIIb, NT-proBNP >8500 ng/L (Wechalekar)]]. This European stage IIIb has since defined trial eligibility. The revised Mayo 2012 system added the size of the clone (dFLC) [[Revised Mayo 2012 staging (Kumar)]], and a BNP-based version serves centres without NT-proBNP [[Boston University BNP staging (Lilleness)]]. The kidney has its own stages, built on proteinuria and eGFR [[Renal staging and response (Palladini)]].</p>
<p>The clone also carries prognosis. More than 10% marrow plasma cells carried an outlook similar to overt myeloma [[Marrow plasma cells >10% (Kourelis)]], and t(11;14), the commonest cytogenetic abnormality, was associated with shorter survival with bortezomib-dexamethasone, while the myeloma high-risk abnormalities were not [[t(11;14) and bortezomib (Bochtler)]]. In the daratumumab era, two newer tools refine cardiac risk. The AL International Staging System adds echocardiographic strain and defines a stage IIIC, about 5% of patients, with a median survival of 7 months [[AL-ISS with stage IIIC (Khwaja)]]. Extracellular volume on cardiac MRI measures the amyloid load and suggests that the depth of response needed for survival rises with it [[Extracellular volume by cardiac MRI (Porcari)]].</p>

<h3>Response: how deep and how fast</h3>
<p>The 2005 consensus gave the field a common language for organ involvement [[Consensus organ involvement and response criteria (Gertz)]]. The 2012 criteria, validated against survival in 816 patients, defined complete response, very good partial response (dFLC below 40 mg/L) and partial response, and showed that free light chain and NT-proBNP changes predict survival as early as 3 months [[Hematologic and cardiac response criteria (Palladini)]]. They made early response the reason to change treatment. Cardiac response is now graded: 5-year overall survival was 93%, 79%, 65% and 33% for cardiac complete, very good partial, partial and no response [[Graded cardiac response criteria (Muchtar)]]. Even complete hematologic response may leave minimal residual disease, found in 54% of complete responders and associated with fewer organ responses [[MRD by next-generation flow (Palladini)]].</p>

<h3>First-line treatment: from melphalan to daratumumab</h3>
<p>Oral melphalan with high-dose dexamethasone gave hematologic responses in 67% of patients ineligible for transplantation [[Melphalan-dexamethasone (Palladini)]]. High-dose melphalan with transplantation gave a complete response in 40% and a median survival of 4.6 years in selected patients, at the cost of 13% mortality within 100 days [[High-dose melphalan and transplantation, 8-year series (Skinner)]]. The only randomized comparison found no advantage for transplantation: median survival was 22.2 months against 56.9 months with melphalan-dexamethasone [[High-dose melphalan vs MDex (Jaccard)]]. Transplantation has since become safer, with 100-day mortality falling from 20% to 5% in North America [[CIBMTR transplantation trends (D'Souza)]], and a small randomized trial suggested that bortezomib induction before it deepens response [[Bortezomib-dexamethasone induction before transplantation (Huang)]]. Whether transplantation adds to modern induction is being tested again.</p>
<p>Bortezomib changed the backbone. CyBorD gave a hematologic response in 60% of 230 patients, but only 42% in stage IIIb [[CyBorD, European study (Palladini)]]. Adding bortezomib to melphalan-dexamethasone raised the 3-month response rate from 52% to 79% and halved mortality, the first survival gain in a controlled trial [[BMDex vs MDex (Kastritis)]]. ANDROMEDA then added subcutaneous daratumumab to VCd: hematologic complete response rose from 18.1% to 53.3%, and cardiac and renal responses roughly doubled [[ANDROMEDA (Kastritis)]]. At a median follow-up of 61.4 months, complete response was 59.5% against 19.2%, and both major organ deterioration progression-free survival (HR 0.44) and overall survival (HR 0.62) were longer. The FDA granted accelerated approval in January 2021 and traditional approval in November 2025.</p>

<h3>Stage IIIb</h3>
<p>Patients in stage IIIb were excluded from ANDROMEDA and from most trials. In the EMN22 trial, daratumumab alone in 40 such patients gave a hematologic response within a median of 1 week and a 6-month overall survival of 65%, with cardiac failure and sudden death as the main serious events [[EMN22, daratumumab in stage IIIb (Kastritis)]]. The US prescribing information does not recommend daratumumab for stage IIIb outside trials, while the EHA-ISA guideline lists daratumumab, alone or in dose-modified combinations, for these patients.</p>

<h3>Relapsed disease</h3>
<p>Treatment at relapse borrows from myeloma and is chosen by previous exposure, depth and duration of the first response, and frailty. Lenalidomide at a reduced dose [[Lenalidomide-dexamethasone (Sanchorawala)]], pomalidomide [[Pomalidomide-dexamethasone (Dispenzieri)]], once-weekly bortezomib [[Bortezomib, once vs twice weekly (Reece)]] and bendamustine, used mainly when the clone is lymphoplasmacytic [[Bendamustine-dexamethasone (Lentzsch)]], all came from single-arm trials. The only phase 3 trial at relapse, TOURMALINE-AL1, did not improve the hematologic response rate with ixazomib (53% against 51%), although time to vital organ deterioration or death was longer [[TOURMALINE-AL1 (Dispenzieri)]]. Anti-CD38 antibodies gave fast, deep responses in daratumumab-naive patients: very good partial response or better in 47.5% [[Daratumumab monotherapy, relapsed (Roussel)]] and complete or very good partial response in 86% [[Daratumumab monotherapy, relapsed (Sanchorawala)]], and 57% with isatuximab [[SWOG S1702, isatuximab (Parker)]]. In patients with t(11;14), venetoclax-based regimens gave very good partial or complete responses in 78%, against 30% without the translocation, in a retrospective series [[Venetoclax, t(11;14) (Premkumar)]].</p>

<h3>Removing the amyloid</h3>
<p>Chemotherapy stops new light chain production but does not remove what is deposited. NEOD001, later birtamimab, was well tolerated and associated with cardiac and renal responses in a first trial [[NEOD001 (birtamimab) phase 1/2 (Gertz)]], but the phase 3 VITAL trial was stopped for futility; a post hoc survival benefit in Mayo stage IV disease was not confirmed by AFFIRM-AL, as reported by the sponsor [[VITAL, birtamimab (Gertz)]]. In the two CARES trials of anselamimab with chemotherapy in stage IIIa and IIIb disease, the primary end point was not met; in 72 patients with κ disease, mortality and cardiovascular hospitalizations were lower, a hypothesis that needs confirmation [[CARES, anselamimab (Wechalekar)]]. Doxycycline added to CyBorD did not improve progression-free survival in a randomized trial [[Doxycycline with CyBorD (Shen)]].</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>Typing the deposit, ideally by mass spectrometry, and testing for the clone with free light chains and serum and urine immunofixation are the start of every case; a positive bone scan does not exclude AL in a patient with a monoclonal protein. Patients are staged by the heart, with the European IIIb and, increasingly, the AL-ISS IIIC. For most newly diagnosed patients, daratumumab-VCd is the standard; transplantation is reserved for selected patients. The goal is a hematologic complete response, ideally with a very low involved free light chain, and treatment is changed early when the response is inadequate (EHA-ISA 2023; NCCN not checked). At relapse, the choice follows previous exposure, with venetoclax for t(11;14). No fibril-directed therapy is approved.</p>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li>How to treat stage IIIb and IIIC disease, where many deaths occur before any treatment can work.</li>
<li>Whether transplantation adds anything after daratumumab-based induction, and how long daratumumab should be continued.</li>
<li>Whether treatment should be guided by cardiac amyloid load, MRD, or t(11;14) status, none of which has been tested prospectively.</li>
<li>Whether the κ subgroup benefit with anselamimab is real.</li>
<li>Which treatment after daratumumab: no randomized trial has addressed this setting.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>BCMA-directed therapies are moving into first-line trials. Three phase 3 trials, not yet recruiting, compare linvoseltamab, etentamig and NXC-201 CAR T cells with daratumumab-CyBorD in newly diagnosed patients, and SWOG S2213 tests transplantation after daratumumab-VCd induction. These trials and the newer agents are on the Trials to watch and New drugs to watch tabs.</p>
"""
